WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.580
Sesame Street, one, two, three, two great games in one, learning fun with your pals from Sesame Street.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:31.100
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.140
I'm Mike.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.260
I'm Big Bird.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.720
I'm Joe.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.880
Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.440
Guys, can you tell me how to get to Sesame Street?

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.360
You're gonna take the J down to Delancey.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.440
Uh, you walk like four blocks.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.060
Uh, that's about it.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.340
You didn't imagine Sesame Street being in Harlem?

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.000
It's in Alphabet City, isn't it?

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.660
Alphabet City? Okay, that makes even more sense, granted the fact that it's A, B, C.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.060
What you should know though is if you pass Sesame Street, you can't turn back to go get to it.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.220
You have to just keep going until you loop around back to the top of the menu.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.100
Oh, you have opened a can of worms on us, Joe.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.360
Like, we're not, we're not talking games.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.820
We're talking Sesame Street, okay?

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.840
Just the, just the world of Sesame Street right now.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.540
We'll get to the game, which may or may not have a menu that makes no sense whatsoever.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.520
Sesame Street.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.240
What's, what's the deal?

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.420
Sean? Like, in general?

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.520
What's the deal with Sesame Street?

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:47.120
Well, if you don't, uh, if you don't know, Sesame Street is a children's edutainment show

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.140
that has been running for decades, and they made video games about it.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.440
I'm not sure what you want me to say.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.560
I think you've covered a lot of the ground of Sesame Street.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.440
Sam, would you mind telling us your personal experiences with Sesame Street?

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.540
I remember really liking the, you gotta put down the rubber ducky if you wanna play the

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.640
saxophone song with Bert and Ernie.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.880
That, I think, was my favorite.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:17.100
And I remember when, I think Elmo had a worm that went to the moon at one point, which was

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.340
pretty cool that Worm did that.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.140
Um. Okay. And, also, they're puppets.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.120
I don't think, I don't know if we said that, but they're-

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.300
Uh, are they puppets?

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.180
Some of them. They're Muppets.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.040
Yeah, I think they're Muppets.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.160
Oh, right, I forget.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.560
Yeah. Which leads me to my final question for Joe.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.260
Joe, The Muppet Show, Sesame Street, same universe?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.760
Uh, well, you got Kermit the Frog in there, and, you know, he kind of crosses over, he

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.260
kind of connects the two, so I think it's part of the, you know, like an extended universe

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.760
kind of thing. Yeah, I'd say so.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.520
So, imagine a world where Disney cares as much about, like, the Muppets and Sesame Street

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.620
cinematic universe as they do say the Marvel cinematic universe.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:04.400
What kind, you know, like, what kind of video game today would you expect from Sesame Street?

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.060
That question just threw Sean off.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.680
Yeah, yeah, yeah. He was like, what do you mean?

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.420
If you saw his video right now, he spit into the camera.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.920
He's taking a sip of coffee, spit it out into the camera.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.520
I didn't see him making a Wii game.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.680
Yeah. Like, a Wii game now?

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.920
They'd make an Nintendo Wii game now for Sesame Street.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.380
Yeah. With the balance board.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:33.480
I want to see, like, a Bethesda-style RPG where you go around talking to all of the Muppets

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.280
and doing quests for them.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.280
Oh, you know Oscar the Grouch is going to have the best quest.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.780
Yeah. I don't know if it has to be a Bethesda-style in the sense of, like, the way that their games

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.780
play out, but I would love to have, like, their kind of dialogue tree in a Sesame Street

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:54.120
game where you just- It needs to be Fallout 3 slash Oblivion era, like, the entire world pauses, and you just

00:03:54.120 --> 00:04:00.700
sort of, like, very awkwardly pan to their face, and they just sort of, like, Oscar the

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.180
Grouch is telling you his life story about how he became in a trash can.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.020
It would be perfect.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.020
Do you have character customization?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.120
Do you want to be a Grouch? Do you want to be an Elmo?

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.720
You can be a Muppet or a person.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.640
Yeah. And it's called Sesame Street Outer Burrow, and the whole time you're trying to figure

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.360
out how to get to Sesame Street.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.020
I love it. And then when you get there, you realize it's been nuked.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.660
Yes. Perfect.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.180
Yeah. Guys, our preschool audience is going to be through the roof after this episode.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.160
We're going to have such an uptick in preschool listeners.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.400
Yeah. I was thinking about that, that we probably, for this episode, should avoid curse words

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.120
like shit and ass and stuff like that.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.500
Yeah. We shouldn't say things like that.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.500
I won't say anything.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.980
So, we're not going to talk much longer about Sesame Street.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.080
In general.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.000
Like, this episode's going to be over sooner than you guys know it.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:05:00.360
But Sesame Street 1, 2, 3 on the NES is actually, as I mentioned in the back of the box, two

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:01.560
games in one.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.540
Wow. What a value.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:10.320
Have we ever seen a game be released on the NES before that wasn't first released separately

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.000
and then joined together?

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.960
In some kind of desperate cash grab attempt?

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:22.300
Okay. But just, like, just as a baseline here, like, the term game here is meant very loosely.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.340
Because it could also have been marketed as, like, 16 games in one.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.760
Yeah. I was going to say the same thing.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.020
I think we should do 16 episodes on this for each game mode.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:37.660
Yes. So, I mean, you could call it half a game, two games, 16 games.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.440
I'm going to go on the, I'm going to err on the side of, like, maybe a quarter of a game.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.340
I think it's a game.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:47.600
I would say that this qualifies, these both qualify as games.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.280
I understand that they might not qualify as two separate games.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.760
But that's probably your one cartridge bias.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:00.280
You probably, had this game been released on 16 different cartridges, as you were suggesting, Joe,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.420
I bet you would think this is 16 different really bad games.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.120
Just because it's on one cartridge doesn't mean that the quantity has changed.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:14.160
I bet you if they released this on 16 cartridges, they would have never made it to number 16.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.540
That's true. They'd have to do it all at once.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:22.040
Yeah. Okay, so, you know, we've got Ernie's Magic Shapes, which teaches you about shapes and colors.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:28.620
And then we have Astro Grover, which is where the 1-2-3 part comes into play.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:57.760
I think the first thing we need to get out of the way, and Joe, you can lead this if you want.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:08.680
Love to. There is a menu system in both of these games, and it is probably the least child-friendly menu design I can think of.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.260
And I can defend myself of that, Sean.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.380
I know you're already thinking, like, what's he talking about?

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.340
Children would love this menu.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.700
But they won't, and I'll explain why after you explain why not.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:19.720
I will. But Joe, you first.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:21.260
Menu thoughts? Well, all right.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:27.920
So, normally, on a normal menu, if you press the down button, your selector cursor will go down.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.840
And if you press the up button, your selector cursor will go up.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:39.620
In this case, any button just makes the cursor go down, and then you loop back around.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.100
Which I actually thought was...

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.680
At first, I thought, okay, well, yeah, you're supposed to have a little kid operating this.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:50.740
It's going to get confusing. But actually, it's like, well, I guess that's the simplest, like, all right, you can't screw it up.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.140
Just you're going to... Any button you hit is going to move to the next one, and it's that simple.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:00.040
And now that I think about it literally as I'm saying this, there actually are a lot of games that do that.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.560
They use a select button to just...

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.460
Like, a lot of early NES games, they just use a select button to move down, and you loop back to the top, and you can't go back up.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.760
So, I'm kind of walking back to my finishing game.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.020
Don't worry, I'll get you covered.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.700
John? Yeah. So, this game has two buttons.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:25.920
Like, they have narrowed down the entire D-pad to one button, and the entire face A-B to one button.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.500
And then, you know, the parents are reserved for start and select there.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:32.940
But, like, this is just...

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.840
This is designing for children.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:47.280
Like, they didn't really know what, like, the level of mastery that these children had for, like, an 8-10 button controller.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.360
So, they just narrowed it down to what they could.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.900
And that means that directionality doesn't mean anything, and it's just move on to the next.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.300
Like, why...

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:03.900
Yeah, it could confuse a child that is smart enough to know that up means up, and then it suddenly goes down.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:08.040
But they're definitely aiming for lowest common denominator here, Mike.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:10.380
Confuse a child?

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.300
It confused me.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.780
Yeah, an adult that plays video games.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.080
Yes. So, here's the thing, okay?

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.260
Imagine this scenario, if you will.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:24.320
Any direction you press is down until it becomes up.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.760
Now, that doesn't make a lot of sense to me.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:33.480
I could appreciate a system that would just continue to go down and then recycle itself all the way back up to the top.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:44.640
But when you're saying that if I press the up button, if I press the left button, if I press the down button, it will just continue to move down until I hit the bottom, and then it becomes an up button, right?

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:46.820
Like, that's the tricky part here.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:48.940
I don't see how that is intuitive.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:52.240
You gotta think about this, though, when you want to think about, like, intuition, though.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.540
So, the up button on a D-pad does not point up.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.140
It points forward.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:05.780
So, to a child who's not aware of that, it points away from your body, depending on how you're holding the controller, normally parallel to the ground.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.260
So, to a child who's not aware of that, they'd be like, well, that's not up.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:10.700
Up is above my head.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.160
That is up on the screen.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.480
It's not the thing that's forward.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.840
But then that's like saying down is just in.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.780
It is. Okay.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:17.800
We've been trained.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.120
We've been fooled our whole lives.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:21.240
But the D-pad...

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.380
So, your only real problem is that it doesn't just loop back to the top.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.540
You know what's actually funny?

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.400
It's like, I have a whole thing.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:31.460
This is bullet point one.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.220
I was just interrupted by Joe.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:34.260
I could have kept going.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.840
Okay, keep going, Mike. Okay.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.400
So, this is a...

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.880
You know, if we're remembering that this is Sesame Street 1-2-3, right?

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:50.940
A game where you're supposed to learn your numbers and presumably also know a lot about shapes to the point where you can build a whole freaking human body in one of them eventually.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:11:00.680
If that's the whole idea is it's a learning game, I think we can trust the children to learn a little thing about the up and down button on a controller.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:04.000
This is the moment to teach kids how to use a controller.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.300
Not the moment to coddle them and say, you know what?

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.320
Just hit the left button if you have to.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.180
It'll get you to another menu.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:19.460
In that case, use the select button because that's what has been defined on the NES as selecting an option.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.140
You want the child to hit that little button with its...

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.280
Those fingers aren't attached to anything yet, Mike.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.140
Like, they don't...

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.300
I'm not quite sure why the left and right button should do anything.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.960
They shouldn't be doing anything, the left and right button.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.840
Up and down, want to move in the same direction?

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:37.440
Sure. That's not acceptable in my book, but if that makes sense, fine.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.120
Left? Left to move up or down?

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.500
It's just one big button.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.800
No, see, I understand that, but we're not teaching the child anything.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:52.720
Yeah, but you want to teach the child how to use a controller that is not...

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.860
Like, this is 1989.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:57.420
Video games are brain rot.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:59.740
They're not supposed to be teaching them how to play games.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:03.300
They're supposed to be teaching them about shapes, colors, and numbers.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:08.500
So, I gotta wait until Sesame Street, up, up, down, down, left, right, left, right, start, select,

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:11.220
until I can figure out how to use a controller?

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.240
That's the game where they'll teach me how to use one?

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:16.980
No. I don't think Sesame Street's your place to go to about controllers.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.800
Yeah. If they wouldn't teach a child about a controller, like they wouldn't just bother to do it in

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.720
a manual, then they shouldn't be bothered to teach a child anything on a video game.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:27.200
They should have just stuck to the books.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.240
I feel like that is...

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.060
I don't know. I guess that's a weird stance.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:34.540
It's a weird stance that if you don't want to teach a child about video games, you shouldn't

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:35.920
teach a child about anything.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.900
No, no, no, no. I'm saying you shouldn't teach them anything via video games.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:43.560
Oh, okay. If you're not prepared to teach them how to use a video game controller.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.320
Like, that's like saying, don't teach a child what the keyboard does, but have them play

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.360
like Sesame Street teaches typing.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:52.500
Mike, the game isn't for the...

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.480
It's not how to teach them how to use the controller.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.920
They have simplified the controller to maximize the ability to teach them about shapes, colors,

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:06.440
and numbers. All right, so you go ahead and you are in Ernie's magic shapes and you're in there and

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.780
you're looking through the menus and all of a sudden you're like, you know what I'd like

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:11.620
to do? I'd like to actually play Astro Grover instead.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.380
Well, for that, you got to hit the reset button on the console.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:15.820
You're trusting a child to do that?

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.680
No, the parent is supposed to watch them.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.000
That's the only way to get back to the main menu.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.400
Or the manual is made for the adult.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.860
Like, did you try reading the manual?

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:27.660
The manual is ridiculous.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.860
I never want to look at it again.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.240
It is like 24 pages. Does it have a coloring book page?

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.020
No, it is a beat for beat.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.300
It's basically teacher's notes.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:46.820
But my point here, and I think you're trying to play this up a bit, as I am, but the controller,

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:50.620
it's neither here nor there, Mike.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:56.800
You're saying it's very respectful of them to not sell a separate two-input controller,

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.280
specifically for Sesame Street 1, 2, 3, where there is one button that does directions

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.500
and another button that does all things.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.200
Why scalp these single mothers?

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.000
You're saying that that's very generous of them.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.600
That's very generous of them to not, like, sell a Big Bird-style controller where his left

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.240
feather does the direction than his right feather does the selecting.

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:17.100
That would be predatory.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:21.660
I think this is where Rob could have really come in handy and saved the day.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:27.620
All right. That's enough about the menu.

00:14:28.820 --> 00:14:31.320
I know I'm right, so I don't really care otherwise.

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:34.080
I know they meant that, but they completely failed.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:37.300
They completely failed. I know you're going to edit me saying that out, though.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:40.020
I'm going to edit you out the entire show.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:44.100
Then you're just going to sound like a lunatic, just, like, arguing with yourself.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.880
So, Ernie's Magic Shapes.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.840
I didn't sign up for this.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:58.780
Shapes? I have to learn about shapes and colors and identifying matching stuff?

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.480
Like, this is a game about numbers.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.840
Well, I mean, it's geometry, right?

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:04.500
